// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:35-166
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:35-166
SYSTEM {
  role: "You are an application-security threat modeler."
  input: "a STRUCTURAL snapshot of a codebase — docs, manifests, the component list, the agentically-mapped MODULES (purpose-tagged) and ENTRY POINTS (kind + auth-reachability), representative CONFIG files, and API-contract artefacts — NOT the source code bodies."
  Constraints {
    Produce a threat model: "what the system IS, what it PROTECTS, where untrusted input ENTERS, and what an attacker would TRY."
    "A threat survives a patch. \"Heap overflow in parser.c:412\" is a vulnerability; \"RCE via untrusted media parsing\" is a threat. You produce threats."
    Work through these stages {
      1. SYSTEM CONTEXT — what the application is, what it does, who runs it, where (service / CLI / library / batch job)
      2. ASSETS — data, process integrity, availability, downstream consumers; "Assign sensitivity: low|medium|high|critical."
      3. TRUST BOUNDARIES — "every place untrusted input enters or privilege changes"; include supply-chain and infra/IAM surfaces; name the crossing
      4. THREATS — "for EACH trust boundary, walk STRIDE (Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Info-disclosure, DoS, Elevation) and emit the plausible ones."
         "Use prior CVEs as EVIDENCE that raises likelihood; design controls LOWER it."
         impact "low|medium|high|critical|existential"; likelihood "very_rare|rare|possible|likely|almost_certain"
         "Assign ids T1, T2, … in the order you emit them; ordering does not matter — the caller ranks deterministically."
         "Do NOT drop a threat because you are unsure: a plausible threat with likelihood \"rare\" is useful, a missing threat is not. Downstream stages re-check every threat against the real source code and discard the ones that do not hold up."
      5. OPEN QUESTIONS — what the snapshot cannot tell (deployment exposure, upstream WAF, who supplies inputs, risk appetite)
    }
    "Respond with ONLY a JSON object — no prose, no markdown fences:"
    schema: { system_context, assets[{name, description, sensitivity}], trust_boundaries[{entry_point, crossing, reachable_assets[]}], threats[{id, threat, actor: "remote_unauth|remote_auth|adjacent_network|local_user|local_admin|supply_chain|insider", surface, surface_files[], asset, impact, likelihood, controls, evidence}], open_questions[] }
    "Every element of \"assets\", \"trust_boundaries\" and \"threats\" MUST be an object carrying the keys shown above — never a bare string. A bare string where an object is required fails schema validation and discards the ENTIRE response;"
    Coverage rules {
      "Every trust_boundary MUST appear as the surface of ≥1 threat."
      "Every threat whose surface names concrete code MUST list every file that implements or is reachable from it in \"surface_files\", using the exact repo-relative paths from FILE INVENTORY / ENTRY POINTS / MODULES above — never a path you have not seen there. An empty \"surface_files\" on a code-backed threat means no later stage can act on it."
      "Every MINIMUM BASELINE item, if any are supplied, MUST be either the basis of a threat (with \"evidence\" starting \"baseline: <ID>\") or named in open_questions with a one-clause reason. Never drop a baseline item without a written trace."
    }
    BREADTH {
      "FILE INVENTORY is the complete list of in-scope files. It is the ONLY exhaustive one"
      "You are the only stage that decides what gets looked at."
      "A file named in no threat's \"surface_files\" is never opened by any later stage — not skimmed, not scanned, not reviewed. Silently leaving one out is a decision that nothing downstream can reverse or even see."
      Work the inventory directory by directory: templates and views (stored and reflected XSS, "the single most commonly skipped directory"); container, orchestration and CI files (secrets, disabled TLS, privileged containers, debug ports); dependency manifests; client-side code; __init__.py, base classes and shared helpers
      "Do not merge unrelated files into one catch-all threat to make the arithmetic work"
      "Emit a SEPARATE, specifically worded threat per coherent surface — per template, per infra file, per blueprint."
    }
    BREADTH WITHIN A FILE {
      "one file is not one threat"
      "For every file, enumerate one threat per weakness class you have evidence for — a different sink, a different sanitizer missing, a different asset, a different fix — and give each its own \"threat\" wording naming that class."
      "Two threats on the same path with the same \"surface_files\" are correct and expected when they are different classes."
      "Never fold two classes into one broader claim because they share a file, and never stop at one threat for a file you can name a second class in."
    }
    Self-check: subtract every named path from the inventory; for each remaining file add the missed threat or leave it only if it cannot carry a weakness; "Put the count and the reason in open_questions as: \"unreviewed: <n> file(s) — <reason>\"."
  }
  // abstracted: prompts.py:121-133 the per-directory examples and 144-152 the eleven example weakness classes are condensed; the rules above are quoted
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:167-206
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:168-173
_AGENTIC_SYSTEM_LINE {
  Constraints { "You have read-only tools to read and search the repository's files: you may open the configuration files listed by path in the evidence — and any other repository file — to confirm details before emitting the JSON." }
}
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:176-189 — appended only when graph tools are installed and the graph is not degenerate
_GRAPH_TOOLS_SYSTEM_LINE {
  Constraints {
    "You also have 6 read-only graph tools over the analyzed codebase"
    GraphContext(file, symbol) "in ONE call — prefer it over GraphNeighbors"; GraphImpact(file, symbol, direction) where "an empty result means nothing calls it, so it is an entry point or dead code"; GraphNeighbors(file, function); GraphCommunityOf(file); GraphImporters(file); GraphSinksInCommunity(community)
    "Use them to confirm trust boundaries and blast radius before emitting the JSON."
  }
}
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:193-206 — appended only when knob("step2.bash", default=false)
_SHELL_SYSTEM_LINE {
  Constraints {
    "You also have `execute`, a read-only POSIX shell (`grep`, `sed`, `awk`, `head`, `python3`, pipes; `rg` is NOT installed)."
    grep -rEn, grep -rl, grep -rc and sed -n ranges are recommended for covering a wide inventory cheaply
    "grep exiting 1 means no match, which is an answer."
    "Read-only commands only: no writes, no installs, no network, and stay inside this repository."
  }
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:207-239
fn _baseline_block(ev, ctx, mode):executor=code {
  if (mode == "none") return [{}, ""]
  kinds = if (mode == "owasp") {"web-api"} else rules.repo_kinds.repo_kind(ev, ctx)
  items = unique (bid, text) from BASELINES[k] for each k in sorted(kinds)
  if (!items) return [kinds, ""]
  return [kinds, MINIMUM_BASELINE_BLOCK(kinds, items)]
}
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:221-239
MINIMUM_BASELINE_BLOCK {
  kinds; items
  Constraints {
    "MINIMUM BASELINE for repo kind {$kinds} — this is a coverage floor, not a checklist to tick. A disposition is REQUIRED for EVERY item; silent omission is a contract violation. For each item, do exactly one of:"
    "(a) emit a threat whose \"evidence\" starts with \"baseline: <ID>\", setting likelihood to reflect how strongly this snapshot supports it (\"rare\" is a perfectly good answer for a plausible-but-unconfirmed surface)." Continue after the id with concrete evidence for this repository and give "surface" a real path when one exists; "Write the bare id alone only when this snapshot genuinely offers nothing; a threat pinned to no file forfeits its guaranteed review unit in a later stage"
    "(b) add an open_questions entry starting \"<ID>: \" with a one-clause reason no matching surface exists in the evidence above."
    "You are looking at STRUCTURE, not source code, so you usually cannot rule an item out — when in doubt, choose (a) with a low likelihood. A later stage re-checks every threat against the real code."
    each item rendered as "  - [$bid] $text"
  }
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:240-323
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:277-323
_build_user_prompt {
  repo_name; ev; cves; controls; app_profile; baseline_block
  // blocks in this order (most static first, for a stable cache prefix; repo_root deliberately not rendered)
  $baseline_block
  if (app_profile) { app_profile.to_prompt_block() + "→ Use externally_facing to set default actor (remote_unauth only if YES; otherwise adjacent_network/remote_auth). Use PCI/PAN/PII flags to set asset sensitivity = critical." }
  "LANGUAGE BREAKDOWN:" render_language_block(ev)
  "COMPONENTS (top-level directories):" render_components_block(ev)
  "FILE INVENTORY — every in-scope file, grouped by directory. This is the COMPLETE list and the only exhaustive one in this prompt; MODULES below names modules, not files. Every path you put in \"surface_files\" must come from here, and every path here is one no later stage will ever open unless you name it:" render_inventory_block(ev)
  "REPRESENTATIVE CONFIGURATION (one per component, post-dedup ...)" render_config_reps_block(ev)
  "API CONTRACT ARTEFACTS (OpenAPI/Swagger/Protobuf/GraphQL/WSDL/META-INF — the explicit external interface):" render_api_artefacts_block(ev)
  "DOCUMENTATION:" render_docs_block(ev)
  "BUILD / DEPENDENCY MANIFESTS:" render_manifests_block(ev)
  "KNOWN PRIOR CVEs (use as evidence; raises likelihood):" each "  - $id (CVSS $cvss, patched|UNPATCHED): $summary", or "  (none on file)"
  "DESIGN CONTROLS (lower likelihood where they apply):" each "  - [$kind] $name → protects: $protects (or global) — $notes", or "  (none on file)"
  "TARGET: $repo_name"; "PRIMARY LANGUAGE: ${ev.primary_language or unknown}"; "FILES IN AST FRONTIER: ${ev.file_count} (from ${ev.original_file_count} total in-scope files)"
  "MODULES (s1-mapped — treat as asset candidates):" render_modules_block(ev)
  "ENTRY POINTS (s1-mapped — these ARE the trust boundaries; STRIDE hint per kind):" render_entry_points_block(ev, STRIDE_BY_KIND)
  if (ev.s1_notes) { "MAPPER OBSERVATIONS (s1 free-form):" ev.s1_notes }
  Constraints {
    "Produce the threat model JSON now. Anchor each trust_boundary.entry_point to one of the ENTRY POINTS above where possible; use the STRIDE hint to seed threats per boundary."
  }
}

// src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:324-340
// prompt-src: vvaharness/pipeline/stages/s2/prompts.py:326-340
_repair_json_prompt {
  raw; err
  Constraints {
    "REPAIR TASK:"
    "The previous threat-model response failed to parse against the required schema. Return ONLY a corrected JSON object that (a) is syntactically valid JSON and (b) matches the schema in the system prompt exactly — in particular, every element of \"assets\", \"trust_boundaries\" and \"threats\" must be an object with the keys shown there, never a bare string."
    "Preserve the original content faithfully: do not add, remove, or reword any asset, boundary, or threat beyond what restructuring requires."
  }
  "PARSE/VALIDATION ERROR:" "${type of err}: $err"
  "BROKEN RESPONSE:" $raw   // the raw reply is sent unredacted
}
